<p><a href="tdsprotocol.html"><img style="vertical-align: middle; " src="../../images/navprev.png"/></a> <a href="tdsprotocol.html">TDS Protocol</a> | <a href="index.html"><img style="vertical-align: middle; " src="../../images/navtop.png"/></a> <a href="index.html">Contents</a> | <a href="multipleprotocols.html">Multiple Protocols</a> <a href="multipleprotocols.html"><img style="vertical-align: middle; " src="../../images/navnext.png"/></a></p>

<a name="oracleprotocol"/><h1>Oracle Protocol</h1>

<p>SQL Relay can be configured to speak the native Oracle protocol, enabling client programs for Oracle (eg. the sqlplus command line program) to access SQL Relay directly.
<blockquote>
<img src="../../images/sqlrelayoracleprotocol.png"/>
</blockquote>
</p>

<p>If an instance speaks the Oracle client-server protocol, then any client that wishes to use it must also speak the Oracle client-server protocol.  This means that most software written using the Oracle API, or written using a database abstraction layer which loads a driver for Oracle can access this instance.  However, it also means that SQL Relay's standard sqlrsh client program and client programs for other databases (eg. the psql, and mysql command line programs, MySQL Workbench, SQL Server Management Studio, etc.) cannot access this instance.</p>

<p>The Oracle protocol module is still under development.  Some of the sections below - SID / Service Name, Login Attempts, Server Version, Password Verifier Type, and Character Set Options - decide which clients can connect to the instance at all, and whether a client that connects is able to read a result set; see <a href="#oraclelimitations">Limitations</a> for what still isn't supported.</p>

<a name="oracleconfiguration"/><h2>Basic Configuration</h2>

<p>To enable SQL Relay to speak the Oracle protocol, add the appropriate listener tag:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>In this configuration:</p>

<ul>
  <li>The connection tag instructs the instance to log in to Oracle as user <i>scott</i> with password <i>tiger</i>, using the <i>ora1</i> database in the Oracle installation under /u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0.</li>
  <li>The listener tag instructs SQL Relay to load the <i>oracle</i> protocol module, and listen on port 1521, which is the standard Oracle listener port.</li>
</ul>

<p>The instance can be started using:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt>sqlr<font color="#990000">-</font>start <font color="#990000">-</font>id example</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>and accessed from the local machine using:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><font color="#008080">sqlplus</font> scott<font color="#990000">/</font>tiger@<i><font color="#9A1900">//localhost:1521</font></i></tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<blockquote>
( <b>NOTE:</b> By default, the user and password used to access SQL Relay are the same as the user and password that SQL Relay is configured to use to access the database. That is, they are the values of the user and password parameters of the string attribute of the connection tag. )
</blockquote>
<p>By default, SQL Relay listens on all available network interfaces, and can be accessed remotely by hostname.  For example, if the server running SQL Relay is named <i>sqlrserver</i> then it can be accessed from another system using:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><font color="#008080">sqlplus</font> scott<font color="#990000">/</font>tiger@<i><font color="#9A1900">//sqlrserver:1521</font></i></tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>The instance can be stopped using:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt>sqlr<font color="#990000">-</font>stop <font color="#990000">-</font>id example</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>Since this instance speaks the Oracle client-server protocol, any client that wishes to use it must also speak the Oracle client-server protocol.  This means that most software written using the Oracle API, or written using a database abstraction layer which loads a driver for Oracle can access this instance.  However, it also means that SQL Relay's standard sqlrsh client program and client programs for other databases (eg. the mysql, and psql command line programs, MySQL Workbench, SQL Server Management Studio, etc.) cannot access this instance.</p>

<a name="oraclelistener"/><h2>Listener Options</h2>

<p>In the example above, the instance is configured to listen on port 1521, which is already the standard Oracle listener port, so no special port choice is needed.</p>

<p>If the server has multiple network interfaces, SQL Relay can also be configured to listen on specific IP addresses.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">addresses</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"192.168.1.50,192.168.1.51"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">sid</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>In the example above, it can be accessed on 192.168.1.50 and 192.168.1.51 but not on 127.0.0.1 (localhost).</p>

<p>It can also be configured to listen on a unix socket by adding a <i>socket</i> attribute to the listener tag.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">sid</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"ora1"</font> <font color="#009900">socket</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"/tmp/oracle.socket"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>( <b>NOTE:</b> The user that SQL Relay runs as must be able to read and write to the path of the socket.)</p>

<p>If the socket option is specified but the port option is not, then SQL Relay will only listen on the socket.  If port and socket options are both specified then it listens on both.</p>

<a name="oraclesid"/><h2>SID / Service Name</h2>

<p>An Oracle client's connect descriptor names a SID or SERVICE_NAME - either is just an alias for the database it wants, taken from its tnsnames.ora entry or built into its connect string.  A real Oracle listener checks that name against the services it knows about and refuses the connection with ORA-12514 if it doesn't recognize it.</p>

<p>The <i>sid</i> option gives the Oracle protocol module the same check.  Set it to the SID/SERVICE_NAME (or a comma-separated list of them) this instance should accept; a client naming anything else is refused with ORA-12514 before it gets as far as a login prompt.  Matching is case-insensitive, and either CONNECT_DATA key - SID or SERVICE_NAME - is accepted from the client, since a client only ever sends whichever one its own connect descriptor used.</p>

<p>Leaving <i>sid</i> unset accepts any SID/SERVICE_NAME a client asks for, which is the default.</p>

<p>A connect descriptor that names no SID or SERVICE_NAME at all is let through even when <i>sid</i> is set, since there's nothing to check it against.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">sid</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<a name="oracleloginattempts"/><h2>Login Attempts</h2>

<p>A real Oracle server lets a client retry a failed login on the same connection - the wrong-password-then-correct-password flow OCI clients and sqlplus expect - up to a limit before it drops the connection.</p>

<p>The Oracle protocol module does the same.  After a failed login it sends the error and waits for another attempt on the same connection, rather than tearing the connection down.  The <i>maxloginattempts</i> option sets how many attempts one connection gets before the module gives up and drops it; it defaults to 3, matching a real server's default SEC_MAX_FAILED_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS.</p>

<a name="oracleserverversion"/><h2>Server Version</h2>

<p>An Oracle client works out how to read the server's answers from the version of server it believes it is talking to.  Several parts of the protocol changed at Oracle 12.1, and a client that is told one version and then sent another version's bytes doesn't recover.  It either waits for fields that never arrive, or reads the next message a few bytes out of position.</p>

<p>So the Oracle protocol module imitates one Oracle version, consistently, and the <i>serverversion</i> option chooses which one.  It accepts 11.2 or 12.1, and defaults to 12.1.  Any other value, including a misspelled one, is the same as leaving it out.</p>

<p>The setting moves everything a client picks a reader from, all at once: the TTC field version the module negotiates, the shape of the summary object that ends every response, the server version and SQL version the module reports while authenticating, the version banner and packed version number an OCIServerVersion call gets back, and the default password verifier type described below.  Both settings describe a server that could really exist.  There is no setting that mixes them.</p>

<p><b>This option decides which clients can use the instance at all.</b>  As of SQL Relay 2.3.0, measured against the module:</p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th> Client 
</th>
    <th> serverversion="12.1" (the default) 
</th>
    <th> serverversion="11.2" 
</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> Oracle JDBC driver (ojdbc) 23.26 
</td>
    <td> logs in, runs queries, reads result sets 
</td>
    <td> logs in, runs queries, reads result sets 
</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> python-oracledb 4.0.2, thin mode 
</td>
    <td> logs in, runs queries, reads result sets 
</td>
    <td> stops responding partway through the login 
</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> node-oracledb 7.0.1, thin mode 
</td>
    <td> logs in, runs queries, reads result sets 
</td>
    <td> stops responding partway through the login 
</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> Oracle OCI 23.26 
</td>
    <td> login fails with ORA-03113 
</td>
    <td> logs in, runs queries, reads result sets 
</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<br/><p>The default serves the three drivers that don't use Oracle's OCI library, so it's the one to leave alone unless a client fails to get through the login.  If it does, note the symptom.  python-oracledb and node-oracledb don't support Oracle servers older than 12.1 at all, and against one they wait for data that never comes, rather than reporting an error, so the client simply hangs.  Setting <i>serverversion</i> to 11.2 makes SQL Relay a server they refuse to talk to, and setting it back to 12.1 is the fix.  A client built on Oracle's OCI library goes the other way: it only gets through the login at 11.2, and reports ORA-03113 at the 12.1 default.</p>

<p><i>serverversion</i> is set per listener tag, not per instance, so there is no single listener that serves both an OCI client and a thin-mode client.  If both kinds of client need to connect, add a second listener tag to the instance, one at each <i>serverversion</i>.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">serverversion</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"11.2"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<a name="oracleverifiertype"/><h2>Password Verifier Type</h2>

<p>Oracle authenticates with a challenge and response exchange.  The server tells the client which kind of stored password verifier it has, and the client picks its cryptography and its session key size to match.  Oracle 11g introduced the O5LOGON exchange, with one kind of verifier, and Oracle 12c added a second, stronger one.  Before 11g there was O3LOGON, which uses DES rather than AES and a much smaller challenge.</p>

<p>The <i>verifiertype</i> option chooses which of the three the module offers.  It accepts 9i, 11g or 12c.  Rather than having a fixed default, it follows <i>serverversion</i>: 12c when <i>serverversion</i> is 12.1, and 11g when <i>serverversion</i> is 11.2.  That's because a 12c verifier arrived with Oracle 12.1 and can't exist on an 11.2 database, and a client that is offered a combination no real server could have may refuse the login.  Setting <i>verifiertype</i> to something the module doesn't recognize is the same as leaving it out.</p>

<p>9i is never the default.  It is the O3LOGON exchange, which is what a client from before Oracle 10g speaks, and it is the only one such a client can answer at all - an OCI7 era client sent an O5LOGON challenge doesn't answer it, it drops the connection.  Set it only for a deployment whose clients are that old, and note that it applies to every client of that listener: a modern client offered a 9i verifier can't log in either.  Run a second listener if both kinds of client have to connect.</p>

<p>Setting 9i is a deliberate step down in security, and it is worth knowing what it costs.  O3LOGON is built on DES and SHA-1 rather than AES and SHA-512, its password hash folds the case of the password, and anyone who can capture a login can attack the password offline.  That was acceptable in 2002 and isn't now.  It is offered because a client that old has no other way in, not because it is safe.</p>

<p>There is otherwise normally no reason to set it.  The default already matches the version the module reports, and none of the modern clients tested against SQL Relay 2.3.0 needed it changed.  Where one of them can't log in, it's the <i>serverversion</i> setting that decides it, not this one.  The option is there so the 11g path stays reachable, for testing and for a client old enough to require it.</p>

<p>Note that this is not a setting about the back-end database.  It describes only the challenge SQL Relay itself issues to its own clients, which SQL Relay's own authentication modules answer.  It has no effect on how SQL Relay logs in to the back-end database.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">verifiertype</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"11g"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>An instance with a 9i listener needs one more thing: a back-end database of its own.  A client old enough to speak OCI7 is too old to authenticate to the same modern database the other verifiertype settings talk to, so a 9i listener can't share a connections tag with an 11g or 12c one.  Give it a separate instance, with its own connections tag pointing at a database old enough for the client to log in to.</p>

<p>The shape of that instance is otherwise ordinary: a listener tag with verifiertype="9i", and a connections tag pointing at the older backend, as shown below.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example9i"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1522"</font> <font color="#009900">serverversion</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"11.2"</font> <font color="#009900">verifiertype</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"9i"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/9.2.0;oracle_sid=ora2"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<a name="oraclebigchunkclr"/><h2>Big Chunk CLR Framing</h2>

<p>Oracle's wire protocol encodes a value over 252 bytes as a "long form" clr, split into chunks.  Each chunk's length is framed one of two ways: as a single raw byte, which tops out at 255, or, for a "big chunk", as a count prefix followed by a ub4.  Which framing a session uses is negotiated: the module advertises a capability bit at login, and then frames chunks as big chunks only for a TTI 6 client that answers back with that same bit.  The TTI version matters because a TTI 5 session is never sent the capability arrays at all, so its client can never have seen the module's bit.</p>

<p>The <i>bigchunkclr</i> option overrides that negotiation.  Leaving it out, or setting it to "auto", keeps the negotiation just described, and that is what every client measured wants except one.  Oracle's OCI 23.26, ojdbc 23.26, python-oracledb, node-oracledb, and go-ora v2 and v3 all negotiate the bit and get the framing they expect.</p>

<p>go-ora v1 is the exception.  It advertises the same capability bit the others do, but never implemented big chunks, so it frames raw bytes regardless of what it just advertised.  Nothing on the wire distinguishes it from go-ora v2, which advertises the identical bit and does implement big chunks, so there's no gate the module can apply automatically that serves both.  <i>bigchunkclr</i> exists to say which one a given listener is talking to.</p>

<p><i>bigchunkclr</i> is set per listener tag, the same way <i>serverversion</i> and <i>verifiertype</i> are.  Setting it to "off" (or "no", "false", or "0") stops the module from advertising the capability bit at all, and always frames raw bytes, whatever the client answers - this is the setting a go-ora v1 deployment needs.  Setting it to "on" (or "yes", "true", or "1") frames big chunks for any TTI 6 session, regardless of what the client advertises, for a client that implements big chunks without advertising them.  A TTI 5 session still gets raw bytes, for the reason above.  Anything else, including leaving the option out, negotiates as described above.</p>

<p>Note that "off" clears the capability bit itself, not just the module's per-session choice of framing.  ojdbc decides whether to expect big chunks from that bit alone, so a listener that kept the bit advertised while framing raw bytes would break ojdbc instead of fixing go-ora v1.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">bigchunkclr</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"off"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<a name="oraclecharsets"/><h2>Character Set Options</h2>

<p>While negotiating the protocol, the Oracle protocol module tells the client which character sets to expect.  Oracle identifies character sets by number, and the client uses those numbers to choose the codec it decodes the data with.</p>

<p>Note that the module only advertises the character sets.  It doesn't convert anything.  The bytes a client receives are the bytes the back-end database returned, so these options should be set to match what the back-end database actually sends, or the client will decode correct data with the wrong codec.</p>

<p>The <i>charset</i> option sets the database character set id.  It defaults to 873, which is AL32UTF8.</p>

<p>A listener with <i>verifiertype="9i"</i> defaults to 31 instead, which is WE8ISO8859P1.  That matches a connection with no <i>nls_lang</i> in its connect string, which hands back single-byte data.  If the connection's <i>nls_lang</i> is AL32UTF8, set <i>charset</i> to 873 on that listener.  Otherwise an OCI7 client corrupts its non-ASCII text, both in the rows it fetches and in the values it binds.</p>

<p>The <i>nationalcharset</i> option sets the national character set id.  It defaults to 2000, which is AL16UTF16.  That is the only national character set modern Oracle databases use, so there is rarely a reason to set it at all.</p>

<p>Prior to version 2.3.0, SQL Relay always reported character set 1, which is US7ASCII, and some clients take that literally.  python-oracledb, for example, maps character set 1 to Python's ascii codec, which raises an exception on any byte of 0x80 or higher.  Such a client cannot read a result set containing any non-ASCII data at all.</p>

<p>If a client still fails to decode a result set, try setting <i>charset</i> to 178, which is WE8MSWIN1252.  Every byte value decodes under cp1252, so decoding never fails, though bytes that aren't really cp1252 will decode to the wrong characters.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">charset</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"178"</font> <font color="#009900">nationalcharset</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"2000"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>Any Oracle character set id may be used.  Ids are listed in the Oracle Database Globalization Support Guide, and the id for a given character set name can be looked up in a running Oracle database with the NLS_CHARSET_ID() function.  For example, to get the ids matching a database's own character sets:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><font color="#008080">select</font> parameter<font color="#990000">,</font>value<font color="#990000">,</font><b><font color="#000000">nls_charset_id</font></b><font color="#990000">(</font>value<font color="#990000">)</font>
<font color="#008080">from</font> nls_database_parameters
where <font color="#008080">parameter</font> <b><font color="#000000">in</font></b> <font color="#990000">(</font><font color="#FF0000">'NLS_CHARACTERSET'</font><font color="#990000">,</font><font color="#FF0000">'NLS_NCHAR_CHARACTERSET'</font><font color="#990000">);</font></tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>Setting either option to 0, or to something that isn't a number, is the same as leaving it out, and the default is used.</p>

<a name="oracledateformat"/><h2>Date Format</h2>

<p>A native Oracle client expects a DATE column to carry a time of day, not just a date.  Without any special handling, that time of day is lost before the Oracle protocol module ever sees the value: DATE columns are fetched using the connection's session-level NLS_DATE_FORMAT, which is typically DD-MON-RR, and that format has no time component.</p>

<p>The fix is the <i>date_to_text_format</i> connect string parameter on the <b>connection</b> tag, not a listener attribute - see <a href="../configreference.html#oracle">SQL Relay Configuration Reference</a> for the full set of Oracle connect string options.  Setting it fetches DATE columns as Oracle's binary date type and converts them to text on the client side, in the given format, so the time of day survives.  It has no effect on the session, and it does not change how Oracle parses date literals going the other way, in inserts or where clauses.  It applies to DATE columns only; TIMESTAMP columns are unaffected.</p>

<p>When an instance speaks the Oracle protocol, setting <i>date_to_text_format</i> on its connection is recommended, for example:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><font color="#990000">&lt;</font><font color="#008080">connection</font> string<font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=exampleuser;password=examplepassword;oracle_sid=ora1;date_to_text_format=YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS"</font><font color="#990000">/&gt;</font></tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>( <b>WARNING:</b> Whatever format is chosen, the result is text that other code then has to parse back apart.  The Oracle protocol module parses it to build the binary date it sends to the client, and so do the C++ client API, the ODBC driver, and the server itself when it needs the parts of a date.  All of them look for one of a fixed set of delimiters, and the sets differ - the ODBC driver, for example, accepts /, - and : but not a dot.  So an exotic date_to_text_format could format cleanly in Oracle and still parse incorrectly downstream.  Stick to /, - and :, and use a numeric, fixed-width format, such as the one above.)</p>

<p>Also note that if the connection's <i>maxfieldsize</i> is set small, it truncates the formatted date text the same way it truncates any other field, since the server truncates any field longer than <i>maxfieldsize</i>.</p>

<a name="oracleauth"/><h2>Authentication/Encryption Options</h2>

<p>When using the Oracle protocol, there are several options for controlling which users are allowed to access an instance of SQL Relay.</p>

<a name="oracleconnectstringsauth"/><h2>Connect Strings Auth</h2>

<p>The default authentication option is <i>connect strings</i> auth.</p>

<p>When connect strings authentication is used, a user is authenticated against the set of user/password combinations that SQL Relay is configured to use to access the database.  That is, they are the values of the user and password parameters of the string attribute of the connection tag.</p>

<p>The Basic Configuration example above uses this option, and configures SQL Relay to access the database using scott/tiger credentials.  In that case, clients would also use scott/tiger credentials to access SQL Relay.</p>

<p>Though it is not necessary, it is possible to explicitly configure SQL Relay to load the <i>connect strings</i> auth module as follows:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">sid</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;auths&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;auth</font></b> <font color="#009900">module</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle_connectstrings"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/auths&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<a name="oracleuserlistauth"/><h2>User List Auth</h2>

<p>Another popular authentication option is <i>user list</i> auth.</p>

<p>When user list authentication is used, a user is authenticated against a static list of user/password combinations, which may be different than the user/password used that SQL Relay uses to access the database.</p>

<p>To enable user list auth, you must provide a list of user/password combinations, as follows:</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">sid</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;auths&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;auth</font></b> <font color="#009900">module</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle_userlist"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
				<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;user</font></b> <font color="#009900">user</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oneuser"</font> <font color="#009900">password</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"onepassword"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
				<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;user</font></b> <font color="#009900">user</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"anotheruser"</font> <font color="#009900">password</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"anotherpassword"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
				<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;user</font></b> <font color="#009900">user</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"yetanotheruser"</font> <font color="#009900">password</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"yetanotherpassword"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/auth&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/auths&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=scott;password=tiger;oracle_home=/u01/app/oracle/product/12.1.0;oracle_sid=ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>When using SQL Relay as a transparent proxy, it is desirable to define the same set of users that are defined in the database itself.  While this may sound inconvenient, in practice, most apps use a single user to access the database, and it's not uncommon for multiple apps to share a user.  So, the list is typically short.</p>

<p>Oracle JDBC (ojdbc) uppercases an unquoted username before it puts it on the wire.  The <i>oracle_userlist</i> module compares usernames case-sensitively, so a user list entry only matches a login sent in the exact same case.  A username that ojdbc clients will use must be configured in the case ojdbc sends it, or configured twice, once in each case, so that both an ojdbc client and a client that sends the username as typed can log in.</p>

<p>There is also an <i>oracle_database</i> auth module, structurally similar to <i>mysql_database</i>, but it only supports the <i>oracle_clear_password</i> method, and no protocol module SQL Relay ships ever selects that method for Oracle-protocol clients - they always authenticate with O5LOGON instead (see <a href="#oraclelimitations">Limitations</a> below).  As a result, database auth isn't currently reachable through the Oracle protocol listener, unlike with the MySQL and PostgreSQL protocols.</p>

<a name="oracleforeignbackend"/><h2>Foreign Backend</h2>

<p>Since SQL Relay supports a variety of database back-ends, the app can also be redirected to any of these databases, instead of the Oracle database it was originally written to use.</p>

<p>This is as simple as specifying a different database using the <i>dbase</i> attribute in the instance tag and specifying the appropriate database connection string in the <i>string</i> attribute of the connection tag.</p>

<blockquote>
<!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight 3.1.9
by Lorenzo Bettini
http://www.lorenzobettini.it
http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
<pre><tt><b><font color="#000080">&lt;?xml</font></b> <font color="#009900">version</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1.0"</font><b><font color="#000080">?&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instances&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;instance</font></b> <font color="#009900">id</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"example"</font> <font color="#009900">dbase</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"mysql"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listeners&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;listener</font></b> <font color="#009900">protocol</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"oracle"</font> <font color="#009900">port</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"1521"</font> <font color="#009900">sid</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"ora1"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/listeners&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connections&gt;</font></b>
			<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;connection</font></b> <font color="#009900">string</font><font color="#990000">=</font><font color="#FF0000">"user=mysqluser;password=mysqlpassword;db=mysqldb;host=mysqlhost"</font><b><font color="#0000FF">/&gt;</font></b>
		<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/connections&gt;</font></b>
	<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instance&gt;</font></b>
<b><font color="#0000FF">&lt;/instances&gt;</font></b>
</tt></pre>

</blockquote>
<p>In this example, an Oracle front-end provides Oracle applications access to a MySQL back-end database.</p>

<p>Depending on the back-end, it might also be necessary to make some other configuration changes, as well, for example:</p>

<ul>
  <li>In the instance tag:</li>
  <ul>
    <li>set translatebindvariables="yes"</li>
    <li>set the isolationlevel attribute</li>
    <li>set transactionmodel="explicit"</li>
  </ul>

  <li>In the string attribute of the connection tag:</li>
  <ul>
    <li>set autocommit=yes or autocommit=no</li>
    <li>implement a function that returns the last insert id and specify it using the lastinsertidfunction parameter</li>
    <li>set the identity parameter to override the database type</li>
    <li>set ignorespace=yes</li>
    <li>set the overrideschema parameter</li>
    <li>set the typemangling and/or tablemangling parameters</li>
  </ul>

  <li>use <a href="querytranslation.html#querytranslation">Query Translation</a> modules</li>
  <li>use <a href="resultsettranslation.html#resultsettranslation">Result Set Translation</a> modules</li>
  <li>use <a href="errortranslation.html#errortranslation">Error Translation</a> modules</li>
  <li>use <a href="sessionqueries.html#sessionqueries">Session Queries</a></li>
</ul>

<p>In your app, it might stil be necessary to modify some queries to use the syntax of the foreign database, or even update some code, but a full rewrite of the app should not be necessary.</p>

<p><b>dbtype=</b> impersonation also affects how numeric result-set columns are reported: when it overrides a non-Oracle backend to identify as Oracle, integer columns and <i>numeric</i> columns are reported to the client as Oracle's <i>NUMBER</i> type - precision 38, scale 0, unless the column already declares a scale - the same as a real Oracle server would report, instead of the backend's native integer or numeric type.  This matters for ODBC (or other API) clients written against Oracle-specific drivers, which may not understand another backend's native integer types and can mishandle them.</p>

<a name="oraclelimitations"/><h2>Limitations</h2>

<p>The implementation of the Oracle protocol isn't 100% complete.  Some notable limitations follow:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Oracle native network encryption and crypto-checksumming (the Advanced Networking Option) are not supported.  SQL Relay negotiates them off.  Clients work normally with SQLNET.ENCRYPTION_CLIENT and SQLNET.CRYPTO_CHECKSUM_CLIENT set to ACCEPTED (the default), REQUESTED or REJECTED.  A client with either set to REQUIRED will fail to connect, with ORA-12660.</li>
  <li>The module always authenticates with O5LOGON (or O3LOGON when verifiertype="9i"), Oracle's challenge/response exchanges.  There is no option that requests a cleartext password instead, and no real Oracle client offers one - a challenge/response exchange is mandatory in every client tested against this module.  The <i>oracle_userlist</i> and <i>oracle_database</i> auth modules also accept an <i>oracle_clear_password</i> method, for a protocol module that might need password-based authentication without a challenge/response exchange, but no protocol module SQL Relay ships - including this one and the native SQLRClient protocol - ever selects it, so it is not reachable through any built-in listener.</li>
  <li>The Oracle protocol module doesn't support TLS/SSL (TCPS) or Kerberos encryption or authentication between the Oracle client and SQL Relay server.  Setting <i>tls</i> or <i>krb</i> on an Oracle listener still loads the configured certificate or Kerberos credentials (and can print a warning if that fails), but neither is ever actually used on the wire - traffic after login stays in the clear, aside from whatever Oracle's own native network encryption would add, which is also not supported (see above).</li>
  <li>A LOB value can be bound into an insert or update normally, but once a client has a LOB locator - from a fetched CLOB, BLOB or BFILE column - that locator itself is read-only.  Getting its length, and reading, opening or closing it through the locator all work, but any operation that would change what the locator points to or create a new LOB through it - write, append, trim, erase, creating or freeing a temporary LOB - fails with ORA-03001: unimplemented feature, and the session stays usable afterwards.  Asking whether a locator is currently open (as opposed to a bfile's existence, see below) also fails the same way.  A BFILE's file name, asked for through the locator, fails the same way too, rather than coming back empty; only whether the file exists is answered (faked from whether the LOB has any length at all, since nothing behind SQL Relay actually opens the file to check).</li>
  <li>A LOB locator is only valid while its row is still the cursor's current row.  A result set containing a LOB column is sent one row per fetch, and the next fetch, a re-execute, or closing the cursor releases the previous row's LOB locators.  Reading a LOB from a released locator afterwards fails with ORA-22275: invalid LOB locator specified.  So an application has to read a row's LOBs before moving on to the next row - fetching an entire result set first and reading LOBs afterwards (for example python-oracledb's fetchall() followed by read(), or a JDBC Clob kept across rs.next()) won't work.  This only applies when the back-end connection is itself Oracle; some other backends send the LOB's value inline in the row instead of handing out a locator, in which case none of this applies.  A legacy OCI7 client (verifiertype="9i") also always gets LOB values inline, even against an Oracle back-end.</li>
  <li>A bound TIMESTAMP, TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE, or TIMESTAMP WITH LOCAL TIME ZONE value is truncated to a plain DATE on the wire: fractional seconds are dropped and any time zone offset is discarded.</li>
  <li>A CLOB character above the Basic Multilingual Plane (for example, most emoji) is not converted to a UTF-16 surrogate pair when read, and the read position is also advanced by the wrong amount afterward - so a CLOB read that crosses one of these characters not only garbles it, but can skip some of the text that follows it in the same read.</li>
  <li>Cancelling a running statement - OCIBreak, Ctrl-C in sqlplus, or a driver's own cancel/interrupt call - does not interrupt it on the back-end.  The module only checks for a break request between calls, so a long-running statement keeps running until it finishes on its own; the cancel is answered afterwards.</li>
  <li>A few less commonly used operations aren't implemented and get ORA-03001 if a client sends them, including: distributed and two-phase-commit/XA transactions, database startup/shutdown, an explicit TTI-level cancel, and DESCRIBE2.  Setting an end-to-end tracing attribute (module, action, or client identifier) also gets ORA-03001, and since that request arrives piggybacked on the client's next real call, that next call fails too, not just the attribute.  Any other function code the module doesn't recognize is refused the same way.</li>
  <li>A fetch asking for more than 100,000 rows fails with ORA-20004, and a batch whose total size grows past 32MB fails with ORA-20005, rather than returning a partial result.</li>
  <li>Legacy OCI7 clients (verifiertype="9i") can only bind NUMBER, VARNUM, CHAR, VARCHAR, LONG and DATE values; binding any other type is refused.</li>
  <li>The listener's <i>cursors</i> and <i>maxcursors</i> attributes bound how many statements the module can keep open at once, independent of anything the real Oracle client is told.  A client that keeps several statements cached and open concurrently - for example python-oracledb, whose default client-side statement cache holds up to 20 - needs <i>maxcursors</i> set higher than that concurrent count, or a new statement fails with ORA-01000: maximum open cursors exceeded once the pool fills up.</li>
</ul>

